---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BlogPostHeader from '../../components/BlogPostHeader.astro';

const title = "What Response Can You Expect From Your Airport PCN Appeal? | AppealAirportPCN";
const description = "A plain-English guide to common replies after a parking appeal, what a cancellation or rejection means, and how to check your next steps.";
const canonical = "https://appealairportpcn.co.uk/guides/what-responses-to-expect/";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "What response can you expect from your appeal?",
  "description": description,
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN.co.uk" },
  "datePublished": "2026-04-15",
  "dateModified": "2026-11-24"
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schema={schema}>
  <BlogPostHeader title="What response can you expect from your appeal?" breadcrumbLabel="What responses to expect" breadcrumbHref="/guides/what-responses-to-expect/" updatedDate="NOVEMBER 2026" fixedUpdatedDate={true} />
  <main>
    <div class="wrap">
      <article class="blog-post">
        <p>What might a parking company say after you appeal? Here are common replies and what you can do next. The exact wording and process vary by operator.</p>

        <h2>1. The company cancels the charge</h2>
        <p>The reply may simply say the notice has been cancelled and no further action is needed.</p>
        <p><strong>What to do:</strong> Keep the cancellation letter or email. If it confirms the charge is cancelled, no further appeal is needed.</p>

        <h2>2. The company cancels but defends its signs</h2>
        <p>A company may cancel the charge but also say it believes its signs and terms were clear. The tone does not change the fact that it has cancelled this notice.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Key point:</strong> Read the whole letter and check that it clearly confirms cancellation and says no further payment is due. Keep a copy for your records.
        </div>

        <h2>3. The company asks for your driving licence</h2>
        <figure class="protected-image">
          <img src="/images/times24-photo-id-request-example.webp" alt="Redacted Times24 email asking for the photo ID section of a driving licence" width="984" height="768" loading="lazy" draggable="false" />
          <figcaption>Redacted. Name, PCN number and date removed.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Some operators reply to an appeal by asking for a copy of your driving licence. This example is from Times24 at Gatwick. It says it wants to check the driver against images it holds. It puts the notice on hold for 14 days.</p>
        <p>This is not a decision. You do not have to send ID.</p>
        <p><strong>What to do:</strong> Check the deadline in the email. Our guide covers what the Code says and gives a short reply: <a href="/guides/driving-licence-request/">What to do if a parking company asks for your driving licence</a>.</p>

        <h2>4. The company rejects your appeal</h2>
        <p>A rejection is not necessarily the end. Read the reasons and check whether your letter includes details of a second-stage appeal.</p>
        <p>If the operator is a BPA member, it may provide a POPLA code. Our guide explains <a href="/guides/what-is-popla/">how to make a POPLA appeal</a>.</p>
        <p>If the operator is an IPC member, the second-stage service is the IAS. Our guide explains <a href="/guides/what-is-the-ias/">how to appeal to the IAS</a>. Check your letter to see which one applies.</p>
        <p>Use the deadline and instructions in the rejection letter. You can also read about <a href="/guides/what-is-pofa-and-why-it-matters/">POFA</a>, <a href="/guides/bpa-code-of-practice-signage/">signage</a> and <a href="/guides/five-minute-consideration-period/">the consideration period</a> when deciding what points apply to your case.</p>

        <h3>Example: a rejection after you refuse to name the driver</h3>
        <figure class="protected-image">
          <img src="/images/times24-rejection-letter-example.webp" alt="Redacted Times24 rejection letter citing Elliott v Loake and offering a reduced payment" width="815" height="768" loading="lazy" draggable="false" />
          <figcaption>Redacted. Name, PCN number, vehicle and dates removed.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>If you do not send ID, the next reply may be a rejection like this one from Times24 at Gatwick. It says:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The notice was correctly issued.</li>
          <li>The charge is not being pursued under Schedule 4 of the Protection of Freedoms Act 2012. So it is not relying on the keeper liability rules.</li>
          <li>Without driver details, it relies on Elliott v Loake to decide who was driving, on the balance of probabilities.</li>
          <li>The reduced payment is extended by a further 14 days. After that, only the full charge is offered.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The letter then says this is the end of its internal appeals. You can appeal to the IAS. It says you must do so within 28 days of the date of the letter, and that you lose the reduced rate if you appeal. Check the date on your own letter.</p>
        <p>The Code requires operators to extend the reduced rate by 14 days after a rejection. It also requires them to say you lose the reduced rate if you go to an appeals service (8.1.2).</p>
        <p>Read more: <a href="/guides/elliott-v-loake-why-it-doesnt-make-you-liable-as-the-keeper/">Elliott v Loake: why it doesn't make you liable as the keeper</a> and <a href="/guides/what-is-the-ias/">how to appeal to the IAS</a>.</p>

        <h2>5. You do not get a reply</h2>
        <p>Check the instructions on your notice. The Single Code of Practice says operators must respond to an appeal within 28 days. If they cannot decide in that time, they must acknowledge the appeal and say when they will conclude it (8.4.2). But not receiving a reply does not automatically give you a POPLA code. Contact the operator in writing, keep a copy, and ask how to proceed if 28 days have passed.</p>

        <div class="disclaimer">
          <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> This article is for information only and does not constitute legal advice. Response wording varies by operator. For complex cases, consult a solicitor or Citizens Advice.
        </div>

        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>POPLA, "Parking charges: understanding grace periods and appeal deadlines" (appeal to the operator within 28 days; POPLA within 28 days of rejection; ten-digit code; do not pay first). <strong>Primary.</strong> <a href="https://www.popla.co.uk/news/parking-charges-understanding-grace-periods-and-appeal-deadlines" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">POPLA</a></li>
          <li>POPLA FAQs (code from the operator on request if you appealed within 28 days). <strong>Primary.</strong> <a href="https://popla.co.uk/faqs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">POPLA FAQs</a></li>
          <li>POPLA home page (if you delay payment after losing, the operator may add charges or use debt collection). <strong>Primary.</strong> <a href="https://popla.co.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">POPLA</a></li>
          <li>Private Parking Sector Single Code of Practice, version 1.1. Clauses 8.1.2 and 8.4.2. <strong>Primary.</strong> <a href="https://irp.cdn-website.com/262226a6/files/uploaded/sector+single+Code+of+Practice+Version+1.1+130426-2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">IPC-hosted PDF</a></li>
          <li>Times24 UK Limited, reply requesting photo ID, Gatwick North Terminal. <strong>Primary.</strong> Redacted copy on this site.</li>
          <li>Times24 UK Limited, rejection letter, Gatwick North Terminal. <strong>Primary.</strong> Redacted copy on this site.</li>
          <li>Independent Appeals Service, "Before you appeal". <strong>Primary.</strong> <a href="https://theias.org/before-you-appeal" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">theias.org/before-you-appeal</a></li>
        </ol>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .protected-image img {
    display: block;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    pointer-events: none;
    user-select: none;
    -webkit-user-select: none;
  }
  .protected-image figcaption {
    margin-top: .5rem;
    color: var(--light);
    font-size: .85rem;
  }
</style>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll('.protected-image img').forEach((image) => {
    image.addEventListener('contextmenu', (event) => event.preventDefault());
    image.addEventListener('dragstart', (event) => event.preventDefault());
  });
</script>
